  "query_preview": "..Satellite imagery this afternoon and evening has\nbeen visually impressive as a strong closed low circulates and \nmoves southward off of the west coast. This system continues to\nbe the main story in Utah's weather through the work week. Late \nthis evening, isolated showers will develop primarily across\nhigher terrain and the northern Wasatch Front, but will be largely\nunimpactful with little to no QPF expected. \n\nAs the aforementioned closed low begins to inch closer late \nTuesday, increasing southwest flow will draw moisture of tropical \norigins northward into southern Utah. Alongside upper-level \ndiffluence and jet support, precipitation chances will increase, \nparticularly early Wednesday morning across southern Utah. Models \nhave trended earlier with the onset of this precipitation, with \nmore widespread showers beginning after 6AM Wednesday across the \nsouth, expanding into northern Utah as early as mid-afternoon. \n\nThunderstorms on Wednesday have the potential to be a bit more\norganized across southern Utah. Given a favorable environment with\nbetter instability, moisture, and shear in place ahead of the \napproaching system, gusty winds and hail will be the main threats.\nHowever, with southeasterly to easterly surface flow veering \nto southwesterly not far above the surface, a tornado cannot be \nruled out, especially considering low LCLs.\n\nWidespread precipitation will continue through Thursday as the\nclosed low slowly pushes eastward. Flash flooding will remain a\nthreat, particularly for prone areas such as slot canyons and\ndry washes. By Friday morning, cold air will push into the state \nas the trough axis passes overhead. Snow levels will drop to \n7500-9000ft as 700-mb temperatures dip to -3C to -6C by late \nFriday. As such, snow accumulations of 3-6 inches above 9000ft \nacross the northern mountains are expected, with perhaps a bit \nmore across higher elevations in the Wasatch and Uintas.\n\nWhile forecast confidence heading into the weekend is still low,\nmodel guidance suggests warmer and drier weather to come.",
